math)\nciteturn2search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Dec 22 2022 math.CO arXiv:2212.10586v2 ... ~Vindas-Meléndez and Yan Zhuang}, title = {{A} combinatorial proof of a symmetry for a refinement of the {N}arayana numbers}, year = {2022}, eprint = {2212.10586}, note = {arXiv:2212.10586v2} }[Button: Copy Citation] ... This paper has been withdrawn by the author due to a crucial error in the proof of Theorem 1.\n\n10 results for au:Labelle_G in:math\n\nShow all abstracts\n\n  * A combinatorial proof of a symmetry for a refinement of the Narayana numbers\n\nMiklós Bóna, Stoyan Dimitrov, Gilbert Labelle, Yifei Li, Joseph Pappe, Andrés R. Vindas-Meléndez, Yan Zhuang\n\nDec 22 2022 math.CO arXiv:2212.10586v2\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2212.10586, author = {Miklós Bóna and Stoyan Dimitrov and Gilbert Labelle and Yifei Li and Joseph Pappe and Andrés R.~Vindas-Meléndez and Yan Zhuang}, title = {{A} combinatorial proof of a symmetry for a refinement of the {N}arayana numbers}, year = {2022}, eprint = {2212.10586}, note = {arXiv:2212.10586v2} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nWe establish a tantalizing symmetry of certain numbers refining the Narayana numbers. In terms of Dyck paths, this symmetry is interpreted in the following way: if $w_{n,k,m}$ is the number of Dyck paths of semilength $n$ with $k$ occurrences of $UD$ and $m$ occurrences of $UUD$, then $w_{2k+1,k,m}=w_{2k+1,k,k+1-m}$. We give a combinatorial proof of this fact, relying on the cycle lemma, and showing that the numbers $w_{2k+1,k,m}$ are multiples of the Narayana numbers. We prove a more general fact establishing a relationship between the numbers $w_{n,k,m}$ and a family of generalized Narayana numbers due to Callan. A closed-form expression for the even more general numbers $w_{n,k_{1},k_{2},\\ldots , k_{r}}$ counting the semilength-$n$ Dyck paths with $k_{1}$ $UD$-factors, $k_{2}$ $UUD$-factors, $\\ldots$ , and $k_{r}$ $U^{r}D$-factors is also obtained, as well as a more general form of the discussed symmetry for these numbers in the case when all rise runs are of certain minimal length. Finally, we investigate properties of the polynomials $W_{n,k}(t)= \\sum_{m=0}^k w_{n,k,m} t^m$, including real-rootedness, $\\gamma$-positivity, and a symmetric decomposition.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMaximal matroids in weak order posets - MaRDI portal (https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/w/index.php?redirect=no&title=Publication%3A6187341)\nciteturn2search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.8 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; Full work available at URL https://arxiv.org/abs/2102.09901 ... submodularity combinatorial rigidity rank function matroid erection matroid on a graph matroid weak order unique maximal matroid weakly saturated sequence ... We consider the problem of determining when there exists a unique maximal {\\displaystyle cX}-matroid in the weak order poset of all {\\displaystyle cX}-matroids on {\\displaystyle E}, and characterizing its rank function when it exists. ...   * Uniqueness of low-rank matrix completion by rigidity theory\n# Maximal matroids in weak order posets\n\nFrom MaRDI portal\n\nRedirect page\nPublication:6187341\n\n\n  * Maximal matroids in weak order posets\n\nDOI10.1016/J.JCTB.2023.10.012MaRDI QIDQ6187341zbMATH OpenOpenAlexFDO\n\nAuthors Bill Jackson, Shin-Ichi Tanigawa\n\nPublication date 15 January 2024\n\nPublished in Journal of Combinatorial Theory. Series B (Search for Journal in Brave)\n\nCopyright license Image Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International\n\nFull work available at URL https://arxiv.org/abs/2102.09901\n\nzbMATH Keywords\n\nsubmodularitycombinatorial rigidityrank functionmatroid erectionmatroid on a graphmatroid weak orderunique maximal matroidweakly saturated sequence\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA combinatorial proof of a symmetry for a refinement of the Narayana numbers — Scifaro (http